Here is the Semgrep rule in YAML format:

```yaml
---
id: vuln-mpxj-80028028
pattern-sources:
  - name: java
    pattern: |
      (private static void processZipStream(File dir, InputStream inputStream) throws IOException
       {
         String canonicalDestinationDirPath = dir.getCanonicalPath();
         ZipInputStream zip = new ZipInputStream(inputStream);
         while (true)
          {
            ZipEntry entry = zip.getNextEntry();
            if (entry == null)
             {
               break;
             }

            File file = new File(dir, entry.getName());

            String canonicalDestinationFile = file.getCanonicalPath();
            VARIABLE:$XSS(canonicalDestinationFile.startsWith(canonicalDestinationDirPath + File.separator))

            if (entry.isDirectory())
             {
               continue;
             }

            File parent = file.getParentFile();
            if (parent != null)
             {
               FileHelper.mkdirsQuietly(parent);
             }

            FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream(file);
            byte[] bytes = new byte[1024];
            int length;
            while ((length = zip.read(bytes)) >= 0)
             {
               fos.write(bytes, 0, length);
             }
            fos.close();
          }
       })
    message: Potential Zip Slip vulnerability. Entry is outside of the target dir.
    severity: ERROR
    languages:
      - java
    metadata:
      cve: CVE-2024-49771
      cwe: CWE-22
      verified: true
      references:
        - https://snyk.io/research/zip-slip-vulnerability
```

This rule uses Semgrep's pattern-matching capabilities to detect the vulnerability. The pattern `VARIABLE:$XSS(canonicalDestinationFile.startsWith(canonicalDestinationDirPath + File.separator))` matches the vulnerable code where the `canonicalDestinationFile.startsWith(canonicalDestinationDirPath + File.separator)` is used. The `VARIABLE:` part is used to match any variable name, and `$XSS` is a pattern that matches any string that is used as an argument to the `startsWith` method, which is a common source of cross-site scripting vulnerabilities. The `metadata` field contains the CVE, CWE, and other information about the vulnerability.

